  "query_preview": "..\n\nMoisture has begun to stream into the area due to\nsouthwesterly flow from the approaching trough off the coast of\nCalifornia. Satellite derived PWATs are around 0.5 to 0.75 \ninches and increasing. High clouds have moved across the region \nwith cumulus clouds forming in western Pima county and along the\nArizona/ New Mexico border with isolated showers and\nthunderstorms. The chance for isolated showers and\nthunderstorms will persist through afternoon. \n\nA sub-tropical jet associated with the Pacific trough will push\nover northern Mexico and drive anomalously high moisture into \nthe area. This will substantially raise PWATs to around 1 to 1.5\ninches, increasing the chance for more isolated showers and \nthunderstorms Friday across the region. Ample forcing aloft \ncombined with strong mid-level flow will provide a risk for \nstrong and organized thunderstorms, capable of heavy rainfall \nrates, damaging wind gusts, and hail. With storm motions likely \nto be fairly quick, any flash flooding threat will likely be \nkept to areas that see multiple rounds of thunderstorms. \nOverall, total rainfall amounts for this first wave of activity \nwill be around 0.25 to 0.5 inches with locally high amounts if a\nstrong thunderstorm or rounds of thunderstorms were to pass \nover an area. Activity will wain overnight Friday through most \nof Saturday. \n\nSaturday into Sunday the strong trough off the coast will move\ninland bringing moisture into the area pushing near\nclimatological maximum for this time of year. This will lead to\nwidespread chances for showers and thunderstorms. Chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms will first increase Saturday night, \nthough there remains some discrepancies in how early this \nactivity will develop. An initial round of isolated to scattered\nshowers/thunderstorms will be possible in the evening. The main\nwave will move in during the day Sunday with chances increasing\nfrom west to east. Though instability will be more limited, \nlong duration moderate to locally heavy rainfall rates may bring\na threat of rapid runoff and localized flash flooding. Rainfall\nprobabilities remain generally consistent with previous shifts \nwith around 0.5-1 inches in the valleys and 1-2 inches in the \nmountains. Due to terrain upsloping, it is possible the \nmountains could see greater than 2 inches of rainfall. A Flood \nWatch has been issued for most of Pima county, Pinal county, \nSanta Cruz county, Graham county, and Greenlee county from 11 PM\nSaturday through 5 AM Monday. \n\nChances for showers and thunderstorms linger through Monday\nmorning but will quickly drop off into the evening. \nTemperatures both Sunday and Monday will be 5-10 degrees below \nnormal, potentially even cooler on Sunday in areas that see \nrainfall through the afternoon. Temperatures will then likely \nmoderate to near or just below normal by by mid- week after the \nsystem fully exits to the east.",
